## Output Folders

Screenshots go in `screenshots/`, plans/PRDs/specs in `plans/`, handoff docs in `handoffs/` (all at repo root). `qa-report/` holds deployed edge function probes only, per the evidence rule under Verification. Routine UI verification writes no file.

Throwaway working files never enter the repo: routine verification captures, scratch scripts and intermediate output go in the session scratchpad. A file earns a repo folder only when a person has a reason to open it later.

## Communication Style

<!-- Optional. Delete the caveman line to keep normal verbosity; keep the report. -->
Caveman mode always active (`.claude/skills/caveman/SKILL.md`). Terse, no filler, full accuracy. Off only on "stop caveman" or "normal mode".

### The report

Every issue and every branch closes with this block, in chat. All five headings always print; write "none" when a section is empty, so a category that collected nothing stays visible.

```
## <issue NN and title, or branch name>

**Done** - plain English, what you can now do
**Still needs work** - incomplete items, each with why
**Bugs** - found and fixed; anything not fixed, with why
**Suggestions** - out-of-scope ideas this work surfaced
**Questions** - decisions needed from the user
```

- **Done** is written for a product owner, not an engineer. Lead with what the user can now do. Table names, migration numbers and file paths belong in a short "behind the scenes" tail if at all. When something went wrong and is already fixed, open with the current state; the story of the defect comes after.
- **Bugs** is not a place to park work. A bug found while building is fixed in that issue's commit. It appears here as fixed, and as unfixed only when the fix needs a product decision, with the proposed fix in **Questions**.
- **Suggestions** is the only home for anything discovered outside what the grill settled. The user does not read issue files, so writing it only into one does not count.
- **Questions** is for decisions, not status. If it can be decided from the code, the spec, or a sensible default, decide it and say so under **Done**.

## Permissions

Always confirm before: `rm`, `del`, `rmdir`, `Remove-Item`, `git rm`, `git reset`, `git checkout`, `git rebase`. Never bypass.

**`git push` runs unattended; force-pushing never runs at all.** Plain push is off the confirm list so an unattended branch can reach CI. `--force`, `-f`, `--force-with-lease` and any `+ref` refspec stay blocked outright, because that push destroys history rather than adding to it. Branch creation (`git checkout -b`, `git switch -c`) is unattended too; bare `git checkout` still confirms, since `git checkout -- <path>` discards work.

**Documents are the exception: deleting one needs no confirmation**, because the words are recoverable from git and a stale one actively misleads: completed issue files, superseded plans, spent handoffs, finished trackers. Delete them as the workflow says, and say so in the commit. The carve-out rests on "recoverable from git", so confirm the file is tracked first (`git log --all -- <path>`); an untracked file has no history and is treated as code. The confirmation rule still binds for anything that is not prose: source, migrations, SQL, tests, config, assets, `.env`, and any directory delete that could sweep those up. When in doubt whether a path is a document, it is not.

**Commit messages:** multi-line via repeated `-m`, never `-F -` (stdin never closes and the commit hangs). A backtick inside `-m "…"` is command substitution and silently eats the word, so drop backticks or write the message to a file and `git commit -F <file>`.

## Verification

Evidence before claims. No "done", "fixed", "passing", or "works", or any paraphrase implying success, without having run the proving command this turn and read its output. A subagent's success report is a claim to check against the diff, not proof.

This governs what you may *claim*; it is not an instruction to re-check your work. Red flags: "should work now", "looks right", satisfaction before evidence, committing or pushing without a fresh run. Where the workflow assigns proof to the user (the pure-style eyeball), say "applied, awaiting your eyes"; that is not a done claim.

**Never pipe a gate command into `tail`/`head` when you will act on its result.** The pipe replaces the command's exit code with the pager's, so `npm run lint | tail -3 && git commit` commits over a failing lint. Run the gate bare, or `cmd 2>&1 | tail -3; echo "EXIT=${PIPESTATUS[0]}"`.

**A check proves nothing until you know what it examined.**
- Print the inspected count beside the verdict, and reconcile the totals (inspected = same + differs + missing). Zero inspected is a broken script, not a clean result.
- Pair every absence assertion with a presence assertion in the same run. All-false in both directions means the check never read its input.
- A fixture selected with `limit 1` that came back NULL makes every later statement match zero rows, and zero rows is not an error. Assert the fixture exists and print it.
- Compare content hashes, never a count of matching diff lines. Read a cleanup back in a separate statement from the delete.
- A checker that reads source as data excludes comments first. Prefer asserting the sites over asserting how many there are: a list names what moved, a number does not.
- Write verification scripts with the Write tool whenever they contain regexes: any script the shell quotes (`node -e`, `python -c`, heredocs) can lose its backslashes and silently match nothing.

**Edits.** Anchor each multi-site edit to something unique, assert the match count before writing, and read the result rather than the source. Never regex an import block; use the Edit tool. Patches must be line-ending aware: a pattern matching `\n` finds nothing in a CRLF file.

**Environments.** A local green is not a CI green when the two differ (an env file, a served font, a running dev server). Before claiming a gate green, ask what this machine has that CI does not, and exercise any fallback path that difference creates.

**Drive the failure path.** A branch that exists to report an error can itself be broken, and it is invisible until needed. Make something go wrong and watch it report.

**Enumerate.** Changing a shared function means checking every caller, not just the one you are working on. Narrowing a value several consumers read is the same enumeration in reverse: list what each consumer *accepts* before applying one rule everywhere. When you harden a loop, carry the fix to the loop beside it. A fix to a privilege hole enumerates every writer, not just the one that leaked.

**Searches.** A search that found nothing has not proved absence: confirm it covered where the thing would live. A search that found something has not proved it is the thing you meant: open the file and find it. An identifier that exists is not necessarily the one in play; resolve a class to the markup that renders it, a key to the row it names.

**Measure every premise**, whoever stated it: a number in a spec, a figure the app computed (check one case against an independent calculation that takes its inputs from the source of record), where a component renders (grep its usage; clear any state your earlier checks left behind). Sweeping a page means sweeping the components it imports. When a value is half of a pair (ink and plate, a badge and its neighbour), changing one half means checking the other.

**Paint is not mechanism.** A screenshot proves rendering only: for a CSS state read the computed style, for accessibility the accessibility tree, and drive the state that breaks it. A green API probe is diagnosis; if the complaint is "the button does nothing", the proof is a click. Prove the operation, not the thing beside it: say in one sentence what would be broken if the proof passed anyway.

**Write evidence to a file only when it cannot be re-derived later.**
- Schema → `<NN>-<slug>.verify.sql` beside the migration, measured numbers as comments. Once applied, the before-state cannot be recaptured.
- Deployed edge function behaviour → `qa-report/<NN>-<slug>.md`: probe output, fixture composition, what the run does *not* establish. Deployed state is not in the diff.
- UI → no file. Drive it, and report what it showed in the report's **Done** section.
- Write measured numbers after the run, never while drafting.

**Acceptance criteria.** Tick them in the issue file one by one before deleting it, and paste the ticked list into the commit body, since the ticked file is otherwise never a commit. A criterion about what a user *receives* is ticked against the surface (generate the thing and look), not the diff.

**A repo copy of a published document is a claim about the artifact.** Before republishing to an existing URL, read the live version and diff it against the repo copy.

## Database (Supabase)

<!-- Keep if the project is on Supabase; delete otherwise. Add the project's own rules below these. -->

- Schema lives in numbered migration files, the source of truth; never hand-edit prod schema without capturing it in a migration. All schema changes go through `/new-migration`. Sort migration numbers numerically, and claim a number only after checking every ref (see Parallel Sessions).
- **RLS: never hardcode role names in policies.** Route role checks and cross-table lookups through SECURITY DEFINER helpers (e.g. `public.is_admin()`). If data vanishes, count rows as postgres first; rows that exist mean RLS.
- **Simulating a user takes two statements.** The MCP connects as `postgres`, which bypasses RLS, so a jwt claim alone changes `auth.uid()` and nothing else. `set local role authenticated;` then `select set_config('request.jwt.claims', '{"sub":"<uuid>"}', true);`, the queries, `reset role;`. `reset role` does not clear the claim. A results table needs `grant insert ... to authenticated` first. Triggers fire for every role, which is why a claim-only test looks valid right up until it is pointed at a policy.
- **`begin; … rollback;` does not protect you in the MCP.** For a write probe, capture the before-state, re-read after, restore explicitly; better, probe a throwaway row you created. A "re-save the same value" probe is a no-op only for the account the value came from.
- **Permissive policies OR their USING and, separately, their WITH CHECK clauses**, so a row can enter an UPDATE through one policy and leave through another. With two or more UPDATE policies on a table, probe every old-state × new-state pair or pin transitions in a trigger.
- "new row violates row-level security policy" on an UPDATE usually means the SELECT policy: the new row must stay readable. If it leaves the caller's read scope, the write belongs in a SECURITY DEFINER function.
- Every SECURITY DEFINER function gets `revoke all ... from public` and a grant to exactly the role that calls it; otherwise `anon` can call it as an RPC. Its authorization check must refuse a null tenant id (`IS NOT DISTINCT FROM` matches null to null).
- **Edge functions run as `service_role`.** A table a function reads or writes needs `grant ... to service_role`, including an existing table the function starts touching. The function authorizes its own caller (an anon key is a valid JWT with no user behind it, so "no user" proves nothing); CORS is not a control. A function pg_cron calls is deployed `--no-verify-jwt` and authenticates a shared secret. Each function bundles its own copy of `_shared`, so redeploy every importer after changing it.
- A gate on who may read covers every way in: table REST, RPC, Storage, Realtime, and edge functions.
- Accounts are never created from the browser; role and tenant columns are minted server-side only.
- Always destructure `error`: `const { data, error } = await query`. Bare `{ data }` swallows RLS failures.
- **Project-wide restrictive policies** every new table must carry (a two-factor gate, a tenant guard): `<list them, or "none">`.

