## Scope, Delegation, Length

**Scope.** Deliver what was asked, at the scope intended. Make routine judgment calls yourself; check in only when different readings of the request lead to materially different work. If the request looks mistaken or a better approach exists, say so in a sentence and build what was asked; never quietly narrow, widen, or transform it. Finish the whole task, and stop short of anything clearly beyond it.

**Delegation.** Subagents are for large, genuinely independent work: a wide multi-file investigation, or a read nobody has done yet (the cold spec review). Never spawn one to double-check work you just did. One agent beats several; keep spawn counts low. The workflow's review gates are a standing request for subagents (`/implement`'s `spec-reviewer` cold read, the whole-branch `review` at `/finish-branch`), so they run even when a session was launched with an instruction not to spawn unless asked. In a nested layout, tell a reviewer that evidence folders (`qa-report/`, `docs/`, `plans/`) live in the outer repo, or it will report them missing.

**Length.** Match written documents (PRDs, plans, issue files, handoffs, reports) to what the task needs. Cover the substance, then stop. Long is not thorough.

## Lanes

Not every task is feature work; route before starting. Pick the lane by observable predicate, never by how small the work feels. When the lane is anything but Feature, state the lane and its predicate in one line before starting. If the predicate flips mid-work (schema gets touched, new behavior appears), stop and upgrade lanes.

| Lane | Predicate | Route |
|---|---|---|
| Feature | Adds user-visible behavior, touches schema, or opens a new module, however small it feels | Workflow below, from step 1 |
| Quick fix | Behavior-preserving or cosmetic; no schema; confined to one area | Fix → `/verify-feature` if behavior was touched (pure style: user eyeball) → `/code-review` if non-trivial → commit on a feature branch. No issue file, no spec review |
| Advisory | No working-tree change intended: audit, ideas, roadmap | `/improve` or `/improve-codebase-architecture`; output is plans, no gates |
| Promote | User picks an advisory plan or finding to build | Enter the workflow at `/to-issues` with the plan path; the plan already is the PRD, don't re-grill it |
| Polish | Visual pass on existing UI | `/visual-spec` or `/mobile-view` loops; user-eyeball rule applies |
| Debug | Something is broken | Investigate first; the fix then re-routes by predicate (usually Quick fix) |

No lane relaxes: Verification, Permissions, the default-branch guard, or `/track`.

## Workflow (Feature lane)

**A branch stops for the user exactly once, at the Go gate.** Everything after it runs unattended and ends in the report.

1. `/grill-me` (new idea) or `/grill-with-docs` (touches existing domain concepts); a handoff doc or brief feeds the grill, answering most questions upfront
2. `/frontend-design` first for any new UI (establish the visual language and record it in the Design System section); `/prototype` (optional); `/visual-spec` for UI work (lock visuals before markup/CSS)
3. `/to-prd` then `/to-issues`
4. **The Go gate**, below. One approval, then nothing asks again.
5. `/implement` per issue. Chains the gates in order: build (`/tdd` for complex logic, `/new-migration` for schema) → `/verify-feature` (behavior changes; pure style skips the browser, user eyeballs) → the cold spec read (`spec-reviewer` subagent) → an adversarial read of the issue's own diff → delete issue file → commit on the feature branch → the report
6. `/finish-branch` when the branch is done: CI trio green, the deep whole-branch review from its merge-base (the only review that sees how the issues interact), docs sweep, merge in the layout's required order, push, `/watch-ci` until green, cleanup, `/sharpen`, then the report

### The Go gate

After the grill and the slicing, post one chat block holding **only what still needs a decision**, then wait for one go. It contains open decisions, each with a recommendation: visual choices with real ambiguity, schema tradeoffs, anything the grill left unresolved. It does not contain the issue slice list, anything the grill already settled, or a recap. If nothing is genuinely open, the block is one line asking for go.

After the go, only two things may interrupt the run: an ambiguity that would make the work useless if guessed wrong (it goes to the report's **Questions**, and that issue is skipped rather than stalled), and the confirmations listed under Permissions.

### Reviews

`/implement` runs two passes per issue: the cold spec read (were the requirements met?) and an adversarial read of the diff (assume it is broken and go looking; a spec read passes code that meets the criteria while being wrong). `/finish-branch` runs the deep pass over the whole branch. `/code-review` is the user's to type and its cloud level is billed, so unattended runs use the `review` skill. When subagents genuinely cannot run, the gate still runs, inline, and the report says it ran inline rather than cold.

**Tripwire.** If three consecutive branches run the per-issue adversarial pass and it surfaces nothing the other gates would have caught, log `per-issue-review-no-yield` to `.claude/sharpen-log.md` and cut it. Reversible on evidence, not impression, in both directions.

### Bugs found while building

A bug found while working an issue is fixed in that issue's commit: not filed for later, not reported and left. It appears in the report under **Bugs**, as fixed. A fix that needs a product decision goes to **Questions** with the proposed fix attached.

Never hand-cut issues while `/to-issues` is callable: a manual slice skips its self-quiz and produces horizontal layers instead of vertical slices.

`/track` auto-triggers on any plan with 5+ steps: checklist in `.claude/tasks/active.md`, survives context compression, no "done" until every box is checked, deleted on completion (git history is the archive). `/qa-sweep` for full-app QA runs.

## Parallel Sessions

Two Claude sessions sometimes share one working tree. Before assuming sole ownership: `git status` for foreign uncommitted edits; read `.claude/tasks/active.md` before `/track` (use a suffixed tracker if it is someone else's); drive the browser through a named `playwright-cli` session (`-s=<task-slug>`) and never run `close-all` or `kill-all`.

- **Never claim a migration number from the working tree or from memory.** Check every ref: `git log --all --name-only --format= -- <migrations-dir> | grep -oE '^<migrations-dir>/[0-9]+' | sort -t/ -k2,2n -u | tail` (paths relative to the repo root holding the migrations), and re-check at merge time. Renumbering a file later is safe on Supabase, which records migrations by timestamp and name.
- A tracker written by another session is a claim about the world: confirm it against `git log --all` and the code before planning from it.
- Check the staged diff for foreign hunks before every commit; commit only your own (`git diff -U0` → filtered patch → `git apply --cached --unidiff-zero`). Never `git add -A` in a shared tree.
- Before merging, read `git log <default>..HEAD --oneline`: a commit you cannot account for is not yours to land.
- To replay a branch, branch off the default and cherry-pick; `git cherry -v <default> <branch>` shows which commits are genuinely unique.
- Merging needs no checkout: `git merge-base --is-ancestor <default> <branch>` then `git push . <branch>:<default>` fast-forwards without touching the tree. When the default has moved, merge in a `git worktree add` at a short path, then remove and prune it.
- A merge that moves only outer-repo files has not changed the app: `git diff <your-commit> <merge-commit> -- <inner>` coming back empty means the tree the suite already ran green on.

## Browser automation

`playwright-cli` through Bash, one named session per task. Log in with the Profile's Login command and `state-load`, never `playwright-cli fill` with a password: it echoes the typed value into the transcript. Never print or "redact" a credentials file; only a script that parses it in memory touches it. Never inline a secret into a command line.

- Where `element.click()` works inside `eval`, React has not re-rendered by the time the same `eval` reads the DOM: use separate `click` calls, or await between. Setting an input's value needs the native setter plus a bubbling `input` event.
- Screenshots: always an absolute `--filename`. From Git Bash, an argument starting with `/` is rewritten into a Windows path; set `MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1` for that call.
